1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.769
I'm Atai. I'm co-founder and CEO of CopilotKit.

2
00:00:03.203 --> 00:00:06.773
In this course, you will learn to use the open-source CopilotKit stack

3
00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:10.176
and the AG-UI protocol, which has been adopted by Google,

4
00:00:10.577 --> 00:00:12.879
Microsoft, Amazon, LangChain, Oracle,

5
00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:15.782
and many of the other leading agentic ecosystems.

6
00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:17.917
So everything you learn here can

7
00:00:17.917 --> 00:00:20.186
apply across the entire popular agentic stack.

8
00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:23.790
In this lesson, you will learn about the Generative UI spectrum

9
00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:27.193
and about its three key pillars: controlled, declarative,

10
00:00:27.494 --> 00:00:29.763
all the way to fully open-ended Generative UI.

11
00:00:29.963 --> 00:00:31.564
And about what makes each one of

12
00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:33.366
those parts different and when to apply each.

13
00:00:33.666 --> 00:00:35.635
By the end of this lesson, you'll have the

14
00:00:35.635 --> 00:00:37.604
mental model for everything you're going to be building

15
00:00:37.604 --> 00:00:41.307
in this course. So let's go. All right, let's jump in.

16
00:00:41.408 --> 00:00:42.742
I want to start the course

17
00:00:42.742 --> 00:00:44.310
with a bit of a provocative statement.

18
00:00:44.411 --> 00:00:47.614
Over the next few years, all UI will become AI.

19
00:00:48.014 --> 00:00:50.617
Meaning every interaction between humans and technology

20
00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:53.019
will be increasingly mediated by agentic systems.

21
00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:55.622
Of course, this is true of complex software products,

22
00:00:55.622 --> 00:00:57.724
HubSpot, Zendesk, Figma and so on.

23
00:00:57.724 --> 00:00:59.726
But it may even be true of your refrigerator.

24
00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:01.628
That might sound a little bit preposterous,

25
00:01:01.628 --> 00:01:03.630
but wouldn't it be nice if you could walk by your refrigerator

26
00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.833
and tell it to order the missing ingredients for tonight's lasagna.

27
00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:10.837
So that's where we're going. Ubiquitous interactions between agents and humans.

28
00:01:11.271 --> 00:01:13.773
But where are we now? Current agentic interfaces

29
00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:16.242
look a lot like the MS-DOS command line interfaces

30
00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:19.045
of the 1980s. These initial raw interfaces

31
00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:21.047
are good enough for early adopter types

32
00:01:21.047 --> 00:01:23.049
who drive the lion's share of initial use.

33
00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:25.652
But they're not good enough for mainstream mass adoption.

34
00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:28.855
To continue this analogy, today's agents are beginning to graduate

35
00:01:28.855 --> 00:01:31.257
from the MS-DOS era to the Windows and Mac era.

36
00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:33.193
If you're paying attention, you've likely noticed

37
00:01:33.193 --> 00:01:35.061
your customers are asking for this loudly.

38
00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:37.063
In this course, we will walk you

39
00:01:37.063 --> 00:01:39.265
through the concepts behind building fullstack agentic applications,

40
00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:43.536
which are ready for mass adoption. Great Fullstack Agentic Applications

41
00:01:43.536 --> 00:01:46.639
leverage generative UI capabilities to build a great user experience

42
00:01:46.639 --> 00:01:48.608
for working alongside agents.

43
00:01:48.708 --> 00:01:50.910
For example, in Claude Code and in Cursor,

44
00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.213
we're able to seamlessly work alongside our agent

45
00:01:53.513 --> 00:01:56.216
and see what it is doing and steer it in coherent steps.

46
00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:58.918
In Notion, our agents are able to take actions

47
00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:01.921
on our behalf in the application right as we work.

48
00:02:02.222 --> 00:02:04.224
In Manus, our agents stream to us

49
00:02:04.224 --> 00:02:06.226
what they're doing and present it with a nice UI.

50
00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:08.461
So why is building all of this so hard?

51
00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:11.564
Developers who first approach agentic application development

52
00:02:11.865 --> 00:02:13.533
start by focusing on the agent itself.

53
00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:15.835
They initially expect that once they build the agent,

54
00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:17.403
they'll just throw it behind an API

55
00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:18.838
and connect it to any user-facing application.

56
00:02:19.239 --> 00:02:21.541
Just like they've done with every front-end and back-end development

57
00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:24.544
before agents came along. But when we try to do that,

58
00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:27.547
we run into a second and initially unexpected set of problems.

59
00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:29.549
We discover that agents break

60
00:02:29.749 --> 00:02:31.851
the Request/Response Paradigm, which has been

61
00:02:31.985 --> 00:02:34.287
empowering the internet for the last 30 years.

62
00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:36.389
Agents are of course just software.

63
00:02:36.456 --> 00:02:38.525
But from the standpoint of pre-agentic software,

64
00:02:38.525 --> 00:02:40.860
they're a little bit like a weird bird.

65
00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.295
They're long running, so they have

66
00:02:42.295 --> 00:02:43.863
to stream their work as they run

67
00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:47.967
and support reconnections, interruptions, and mid-run steerings.

68
00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:49.736
They have to support structured and

69
00:02:49.736 --> 00:02:51.571
unstructured data exchange at the same time.

70
00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:53.139
You have text and voice alongside tool

71
00:02:53.139 --> 00:02:54.574
calls and state updates and so on.

72
00:02:54.774 --> 00:02:57.477
So it's a little bit like implementing Slack and Zoom.

73
00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.612
in a traditional application all at once.

74
00:03:00.113 --> 00:03:02.582
And there's really a long, long list of these peculiarities

75
00:03:03.082 --> 00:03:05.885
that agents have from the standpoint of pre-agentic software.

76
00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:07.453
And so when we try to

77
00:03:07.453 --> 00:03:08.788
stick an agent behind a traditional API,

78
00:03:08.788 --> 00:03:11.791
we end up running into wall after wall after wall of complexity,

79
00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:13.726
and we discover that we have

80
00:03:13.726 --> 00:03:15.595
to reimplement 30 years of glue code,

81
00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:18.398
which we've all been taking for granted for the agentic age.

82
00:03:18.798 --> 00:03:21.000
And that's where CopilotKit and AG-UI come in.

83
00:03:21.201 --> 00:03:23.803
CopilotKit provides open source developer SDKs

84
00:03:24.003 --> 00:03:27.207
and a cloud platform for building user-facing agentic applications.

85
00:03:27.407 --> 00:03:29.809
in a way that's optimized for the agentic era,

86
00:03:29.809 --> 00:03:32.312
as opposed to retrofitted from the request-response paradigm.

87
00:03:32.512 --> 00:03:36.482
The AG-UI protocol, which stands for the Agent-User Interaction Protocol,

88
00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:39.786
lets developers who build agent frameworks and harnesses bring their agents

89
00:03:39.786 --> 00:03:43.489
to user-facing ecosystems by building against our robust standard,

90
00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:44.924
which means they conform to a

91
00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:46.593
few simple requirements and everything just works.

92
00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:50.396
This in turn allows developers and organizations to build agentic applications

93
00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:52.165
to build against all of

94
00:03:52.165 --> 00:03:54.200
these different agents in a streamlined fashion.

95
00:03:54.701 --> 00:03:56.903
To situate AG-UI in the landscape,

96
00:03:56.903 --> 00:04:00.206
you have obviously MCP connecting agents to third-party tools into context.

97
00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:03.610
You have A2A connecting agents to other agentic systems.

98
00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:05.812
And AG-UI is the third leg of this triangle.

99
00:04:05.912 --> 00:04:08.181
Connects your agent to user-facing applications.

100
00:04:08.581 --> 00:04:11.584
AG-UI emerged from our initial partnership with LangChain and Crew AI.

101
00:04:11.684 --> 00:04:14.387
And since we launched the protocol, it's been widely adopted

102
00:04:14.387 --> 00:04:15.955
by many of the top leaders in the agentic space.

103
00:04:15.955 --> 00:04:18.958
Which means that everything you learn in this course can be applied

104
00:04:18.958 --> 00:04:21.361
to any agentic system you work with out there in production.

105
00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:24.664
By the way, if you do come across a system that is not yet supported,

106
00:04:24.664 --> 00:04:27.267
or if your organization made its own agentic system,

107
00:04:27.267 --> 00:04:30.670
you can also build your own AG-UI connectors for that system.

108
00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:32.338
However, we will not cover that

109
00:04:32.338 --> 00:04:33.973
side of the equation in this course.

110
00:04:34.274 --> 00:04:37.644
So you can use AG-UI to connect any agent to your users.

111
00:04:37.944 --> 00:04:39.512
But you can also use AG-UI

112
00:04:39.512 --> 00:04:41.347
to connect any user to your agents.

113
00:04:41.648 --> 00:04:44.517
Everything you will learn in this course can be applied

114
00:04:44.517 --> 00:04:47.654
to building agentic applications in any front end your users use.

115
00:04:47.754 --> 00:04:50.556
From web to mobile to Slack to text messaging and so on.

116
00:04:50.857 --> 00:04:52.659
In this course, we're going to focus on web,

117
00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:56.362
and specifically on React, which is the dominant web framework.

118
00:04:56.663 --> 00:04:58.498
So having laid out the architecture, let's bring

119
00:04:58.498 --> 00:05:00.566
our attention back to the world of generative UI.

120
00:05:00.566 --> 00:05:03.336
First, what is generative UI?

121
00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:07.340
Generative UI is a UI paradigm which is both enabled by

122
00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:10.743
and exists in service of LLM systems and agents.

123
00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:14.013
What does that mean? Well, LLM systems and agents

124
00:05:14.113 --> 00:05:16.916
introduce both new capabilities and new challenges

125
00:05:17.016 --> 00:05:19.619
to the software landscape. Generative UI

126
00:05:19.719 --> 00:05:22.522
is the UI paradigm that takes advantage of those new capabilities,

127
00:05:22.588 --> 00:05:26.092
both to improve experiences and to address those new challenges.

128
00:05:26.693 --> 00:05:28.795
Generative UI solutions exist on a spectrum

129
00:05:28.895 --> 00:05:31.097
where control flows from the developer to the agent.

130
00:05:31.397 --> 00:05:33.099
There are three pillars in this spectrum:

131
00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:35.068
Controlled generative UI,

132
00:05:35.168 --> 00:05:37.370
Declarative generative UI,

133
00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:39.372
and open-ended generative UI.

134
00:05:39.772 --> 00:05:41.374
The entire generative UI spectrum

135
00:05:41.407 --> 00:05:43.710
is needed in modern agentic applications.

136
00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:47.413
Because different solutions along the spectrum have different pros and cons,

137
00:05:47.513 --> 00:05:49.816
and so are best suited for different use cases.

138
00:05:50.116 --> 00:05:52.018
In this lesson, we will briefly outline

139
00:05:52.018 --> 00:05:54.220
the three pillars of the Generative UI Spectrum.

140
00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:56.022
And then, in the rest of this

141
00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:57.724
course, you will dive deep into each one

142
00:05:58.024 --> 00:05:59.926
and learn how and when to apply it.

143
00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:02.762
Controlled Generative UI is a paradigm

144
00:06:02.762 --> 00:06:04.464
where the developer provides the agent

145
00:06:04.464 --> 00:06:06.766
with pre-built, fully custom UI components

146
00:06:06.766 --> 00:06:09.268
that the agent can call upon as needed to augment the interaction.

147
00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:12.271
It allows for maximum customization and predictability

148
00:06:12.271 --> 00:06:14.073
and pixel-perfect designs.

149
00:06:14.273 --> 00:06:18.444
Controlled Generative UI also allows for a straightforward development experience,

150
00:06:18.745 --> 00:06:22.048
familiar from the pre-agentic back-end front-end era.

151
00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:26.052
Controlled Generative UI is considered the workhorse of the Generative UI world

152
00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:28.588
because maximum customization and predictability

153
00:06:28.588 --> 00:06:31.891
are important for your product's most used surfaces.

154
00:06:32.392 --> 00:06:34.594
The downside of controlled Generative UI

155
00:06:34.594 --> 00:06:36.896
is that you have to design a specific component

156
00:06:36.896 --> 00:06:39.899
for each interaction you want an agent to support.

157
00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:43.603
And so implementation complexity scales linearly with capabilities.

158
00:06:43.903 --> 00:06:46.205
We will implement controlled Generative UI

159
00:06:46.205 --> 00:06:48.408
using AG-UI and CopilotKit in this course.

160
00:06:48.708 --> 00:06:51.210
Declarative Generative UI is a paradigm

161
00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:53.513
where developers declare a component catalog

162
00:06:53.513 --> 00:06:55.882
of pre-built Lego-like building blocks,

163
00:06:55.882 --> 00:06:58.885
which the agent can then assemble into rendered components

164
00:06:58.885 --> 00:07:01.687
dynamically and on demand for any given interaction.

165
00:07:01.988 --> 00:07:04.690
Under the hood, the agent returns a structured schema.

166
00:07:05.124 --> 00:07:07.927
that represents an assembly of building blocks

167
00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:11.931
along with data bindings containing values that fill in that schema.

168
00:07:11.931 --> 00:07:15.902
The schema and the data bindings are then passed to frontend renderers

169
00:07:15.902 --> 00:07:19.305
which can visualize the structured content as native UI components.

170
00:07:19.305 --> 00:07:21.407
We will soon see an example of this in action.

171
00:07:21.607 --> 00:07:23.910
In consumer-facing applications,

172
00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:26.279
declarative generative UI is most suited for the long

173
00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:28.881
tail of product surfaces where flexibility

174
00:07:29.081 --> 00:07:31.083
is more impactful than perfection.

175
00:07:31.284 --> 00:07:34.487
Declarative Generative UI also excels in internal applications

176
00:07:34.787 --> 00:07:38.791
that generally favor pure functionality and ease of implementation

177
00:07:39.091 --> 00:07:42.061
over a perfectly optimized user experience.

178
00:07:42.462 --> 00:07:46.165
The reason is that Declarative Generative UI cannot be made pixel perfect.

179
00:07:46.466 --> 00:07:50.336
Components have to consists of a standard combination of pre-built building blocks.

180
00:07:50.636 --> 00:07:54.540
Declarative Generative UI is also less deterministic than controlled Generative UI.

181
00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:56.576
The same user query may be answered via

182
00:07:56.576 --> 00:07:58.744
a different combination of building blocks at different times.

183
00:07:58.945 --> 00:08:01.747
And because it requires more computation and generation time,

184
00:08:01.848 --> 00:08:03.850
it is also slower than controlled Generative UI.

185
00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:06.853
The tools we will use here are CopilotKit

186
00:08:06.953 --> 00:08:10.790
and the A2UI spec, which is spearheaded by Google.

187
00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:14.193
CopilotKit and AG-UI are launch and design partners on A2UI.

188
00:08:14.193 --> 00:08:17.497
And AG-UI allows you to use the A2UI spec

189
00:08:17.497 --> 00:08:19.599
with any agentic backend. Further along the

190
00:08:19.599 --> 00:08:21.701
generative UI spectrum, we find MCP apps,

191
00:08:21.701 --> 00:08:24.203
which are an official extension of MCP.

192
00:08:24.203 --> 00:08:27.406
MCP apps are widgets or even fully featured applications

193
00:08:27.406 --> 00:08:30.109
that get embedded inside the AI chat window.

194
00:08:30.109 --> 00:08:33.513
Under the hood, MCP apps are implemented via an embedded iframe.

195
00:08:33.713 --> 00:08:37.817
MCP apps are supported by the ChatGPT and Claude App Stores

196
00:08:37.984 --> 00:08:41.587
and they are deeply optimized for those App Store use cases.

197
00:08:41.888 --> 00:08:43.990
The advantage is that you can bring

198
00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:46.592
any third-party app into a chat experience,

199
00:08:46.692 --> 00:08:48.995
while allowing it to keep its branding

200
00:08:48.995 --> 00:08:51.597
and without having to customize the host app in any way.

201
00:08:51.898 --> 00:08:56.302
The disadvantage is that this constraint necessitates an inherent indirection

202
00:08:56.669 --> 00:08:59.472
in the interaction between an MCP app and the host,

203
00:08:59.472 --> 00:09:01.874
which introduces additional implementation complexity

204
00:09:01.874 --> 00:09:05.878
because you're implementing essentially an app inside of another app,

205
00:09:05.878 --> 00:09:08.447
security challenges and limited customization

206
00:09:08.447 --> 00:09:10.850
outside of the MCP app window.

207
00:09:10.850 --> 00:09:15.850
Additionally, today MCP apps are inherently tied to an iframe implementation,

208
00:09:15.855 --> 00:09:19.458
which means they're less suitable for experiences that exist outside of web,

209
00:09:19.458 --> 00:09:21.260
such as mobile or Slack.

210
00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:23.462
In this course, we will take advantage of the handshake

211
00:09:23.629 --> 00:09:25.831
between AG-UI and MCP apps

212
00:09:25.831 --> 00:09:29.201
to bring any MCP app, including those apps originally

213
00:09:29.201 --> 00:09:32.605
built primarily for the ChatGPT and Claude app stores

214
00:09:32.605 --> 00:09:35.608
into your own custom agentic applications.

215
00:09:36.108 --> 00:09:37.743
Which means that your users can

216
00:09:37.743 --> 00:09:39.612
bring the apps they're already familiar with,

217
00:09:39.612 --> 00:09:42.415
from Salesforce and HubSpot to Figma and Spotify,

218
00:09:42.415 --> 00:09:44.817
into your own custom agentic application.

219
00:09:45.117 --> 00:09:48.120
Finally, at the very far end of the Generative UI spectrum,

220
00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:50.423
we find fully open-ended generative

221
00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:52.191
UI. This paradigm consists of UI

222
00:09:52.191 --> 00:09:54.060
that is fully generated by the agent

223
00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:56.362
on demand per user request.

224
00:09:56.762 --> 00:09:59.065
Fully open-ended generative UI

225
00:09:59.065 --> 00:10:00.466
is often what people have in mind

226
00:10:00.466 --> 00:10:02.068
when they first hear the term generative UI.

227
00:10:02.368 --> 00:10:04.236
This paradigm of generative UI is most suitable

228
00:10:04.236 --> 00:10:06.072
for the far long tail of user queries.

229
00:10:06.272 --> 00:10:09.575
And for delighting users with custom and boundless experiences

230
00:10:09.575 --> 00:10:11.877
that by today's standards are unexpected.

231
00:10:12.378 --> 00:10:14.780
That said, fully open-ended generative UI

232
00:10:15.081 --> 00:10:17.183
requires far more thinking tokens to work,

233
00:10:17.516 --> 00:10:19.719
which is both slower and more expensive.

234
00:10:20.319 --> 00:10:22.521
And it is far less predictable and robust

235
00:10:22.722 --> 00:10:25.024
than alternatives in the generative UI spectrum.

236
00:10:25.424 --> 00:10:28.327
It's currently still in the experimental phase of adoption,

237
00:10:28.527 --> 00:10:31.030
but it represents the full promise of agentic UI

238
00:10:31.330 --> 00:10:32.932
and captures our imagination.

239
00:10:33.332 --> 00:10:35.735
Given the tremendous pace of development in the agentic space,

240
00:10:35.835 --> 00:10:38.137
it will likely quickly become more and more production ready.

241
00:10:38.337 --> 00:10:41.340
In this course, we will implement fully open generative UI

242
00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:43.442
using CopilotKit and AG-UI middleware,

243
00:10:43.542 --> 00:10:46.045
which means you can bring it to any agentic back-end right away.

244
00:10:46.412 --> 00:10:48.514
Let's do a quick recap.

245
00:10:48.514 --> 00:10:50.516
As you will soon see up close,

246
00:10:50.516 --> 00:10:53.319
the different parts of a generative UI spectrum

247
00:10:53.352 --> 00:10:56.222
have different strengths which are suitable for different needs and surfaces.

248
00:10:56.389 --> 00:11:00.292
Controlled Generative UI, the so-called workhorse of the generative UI world,

249
00:11:00.292 --> 00:11:02.795
is the most appropriate solution for the most used

250
00:11:02.828 --> 00:11:04.930
surfaces in your application.

251
00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:09.335
Declarative Generative UI, which is more flexible but less customizable and deterministic,

252
00:11:09.335 --> 00:11:12.438
is most suitable for the long tail of surfaces in your application.

253
00:11:12.638 --> 00:11:14.640
An open-ended Generative UI

254
00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:16.842
is most suitable for interacting with third-party systems

255
00:11:16.842 --> 00:11:19.245
and for the late long tail of your application.

256
00:11:19.278 --> 00:11:21.480
I'm really looking forward to going on this journey with you.

257
00:11:21.781 --> 00:11:24.483
We will begin the course by building a basic agent chat UI

258
00:11:24.483 --> 00:11:27.186
on top of a solid foundation we can continue to build on.

259
00:11:27.486 --> 00:11:30.790
Then we'll go over controlled, declarative, and open Generative UIs

260
00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:32.792
with hands-on examples for each.

261
00:11:33.292 --> 00:11:35.594
Finally, we will close out with additional building blocks

262
00:11:35.594 --> 00:11:38.064
needed to create a great fullstack agentic application

263
00:11:38.064 --> 00:11:39.765
outside of the chat,

264
00:11:39.765 --> 00:11:41.967
including front-end tool calling and state synchronization.

265
00:11:42.268 --> 00:11:43.969
This is a quickly evolving and emerging space,

266
00:11:43.969 --> 00:11:44.937
and I really hope this course

267
00:11:44.937 --> 00:11:46.072
will get you ahead of the curve.
